---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

const oobMarkup = `<!-- The button posts and swaps the FORM area -->
<button hx-post="/po/88213/reject" hx-target="#dialog-body" hx-swap="innerHTML">Reject</button>

<!-- 422: the form comes back, values kept, error wired to the field -->
<div class="bo-alert bo-alert--danger" role="alert" id="err">A reason is required.</div>
<div class="bo-richtext__content" contenteditable="true"
     aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="err">…</div>

<!-- 200: the dialog body gets a confirmation, and the timeline OUTSIDE
     the dialog updates out of band in the same response -->
<p>Rejected — reason recorded.</p>
<ol id="timeline-88213" hx-swap-oob="outerHTML" class="bo-timeline">…</ol>`;
---
<Gallery title="Pattern: inline validation summary">
  <p class="demo-note">
    <strong>Who uses it</strong>: anyone filling a form with more than a
    couple of required fields — worth it the moment a reader could miss
    which field failed. <strong>What "done" looks like</strong>: every
    problem named in one place AND on its field, with nothing to hunt for.
    On submit, if the form is invalid: list every problem field up top, move focus to
    that summary first (not straight into a field — a screen-reader user hears the
    overview before jumping in), and let each link focus its exact field. Composed
    entirely from existing components — the summary box is just <code>.bo-alert
    bo-alert--danger</code>, and every field's error state (red border, red label,
    required asterisk) comes free from the existing <code>:user-invalid</code> styling
    already in <code>.bo-form-field</code>. Try it: submit the empty form below.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <form data-validation-summary novalidate hx-boost="false">
      <div class="bo-alert bo-alert--danger" data-validation-summary-box hidden role="alert">
        <p><strong>There is a problem</strong></p>
        <ul></ul>
      </div>

      <fieldset class="bo-form-section">
        <legend class="bo-form-section__legend">New purchase order</legend>
        <div class="bo-form-row">
          <div class="bo-form-field">
            <label class="bo-form-field__label" for="vs-vendor">Vendor</label>
            <input class="bo-input" id="vs-vendor" required />
          </div>
          <div class="bo-form-field">
            <label class="bo-form-field__label" for="vs-amount">Amount</label>
            <input class="bo-input bo-input--numeric" id="vs-amount" type="number" min="0" step="0.01" required />
          </div>
          <div class="bo-form-field">
            <label class="bo-form-field__label" for="vs-email">Approver email</label>
            <input class="bo-input" id="vs-email" type="email" required />
          </div>
        </div>
      </fieldset>

      <div class="bo-form-actions">
        <button class="bo-btn" type="submit">Submit</button>
      </div>
    </form>
  </section>

  <script>
    import { initValidationSummary } from '@busy-office/ui/js';
    initValidationSummary();
  </script>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Markup</h2>
    <pre><code>{`<form data-validation-summary>
  <div class="bo-alert bo-alert--danger" data-validation-summary-box hidden role="alert">
    <p>There is a problem</p>
    <ul></ul>  <!-- populated on an invalid submit -->
  </div>
  <div class="bo-form-field">
    <label class="bo-form-field__label" for="vendor">Vendor</label>
    <input class="bo-input" id="vendor" required />
  </div>
  ...
</form>

<script type="module">
  import { initValidationSummary } from "@busy-office/ui/js";
  initValidationSummary(); // once — wires the [data-validation-summary] form
</script>`}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Field labels come from each field's own <code>&lt;label
    for&gt;</code> text — no extra markup needed to name a field in the summary list.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>If your app boosts forms with <code>htmx</code>'s
    <code>hx-boost</code></strong> (as this docs site does — found live while building
    this demo): add <code>hx-boost="false"</code> to any
    <code>[data-validation-summary]</code> form. Without it, htmx's own submit
    interception races this behavior's — htmx fetches and swaps the page before (or
    instead of) the summary rendering, so the failed-validation state never sticks.</p>
  </section>


  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Anatomy</h2>
    <ol class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Summary alert</strong> — at the top of the form, listing every error as a link to its field.</li>
      <li><strong>Field-level messages</strong> — the same text beside the control, wired with <code>aria-describedby</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Focus move</strong> — on submit failure focus goes to the summary, so screen-reader users hear the count first.</li>
      <li><strong>Live region</strong> — the summary is <code>role="alert"</code>, so a dynamic failure announces without a page change.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Data contract</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request</th><th scope="col">Returns / on failure</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /form (invalid)</td><td>422 with a field→message map; re-render the SAME form with values preserved and both channels populated</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">POST /form (valid)</td><td>redirect or the success fragment — never leave a stale summary on screen</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--code">client-side</td><td>native constraint validation runs first; the server contract is identical either way</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Two swap targets, one endpoint</h2>
    <p>A validating endpoint answers in two places: the <strong>422</strong>
    re-renders the form where the user is looking, while the
    <strong>success</strong> usually has to update something else — a
    timeline, a row, a list behind the dialog. Point
    <code>hx-target</code> at the form, and let the success response
    carry the other update out of band:</p>
    <pre><code>{oobMarkup}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">Verified by dogfooding the reference app
    (2026-08-17): without the out-of-band swap the success response
    replaced the dialog body with a timeline and the real timeline never
    changed — the action appeared to do nothing. And remember htmx
    discards non-2xx by default, so a 422 needs the
    <a href={base + '/getting-started/htmx'}>beforeSwap opt-in</a> or the
    user never sees the error at all.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Document-level messages — the one thing the summary is not for</h2>
    <p>Some messages are about no field at all. "Posting period closed",
    "this vendor is on hold", "the month-end run is in progress" — there is
    nothing for the user to retype, so listing them in a field summary sends
    them hunting for an input that does not exist. Those get a strip at the
    top of the document instead:</p>
    <div class="bo-alert bo-alert--warning" role="alert">
      <p><strong>Posting period 2026-07 is closed.</strong> This purchase
      order can be edited and saved, but not posted until the period reopens
      or a controller reverses the close.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted" style="margin-block-start: var(--bo-space-3)">
    That is an ordinary <a href={base + '/components/alerts'}>alert</a> placed
    in the document header — <strong>no new component, no new class</strong>.
    A strip earns its place by being about the <em>document</em>; the moment a
    message names a field, it belongs on that field and in the summary above.</p>

    <h3>The test, and why it stays this way</h3>
    <ul class="bo-stack bo-stack--tight docs-list">
      <li><strong>Can the user fix it by changing a value on this screen?</strong>
      Then it is a field error — attach it to the field, list it in the
      summary. "Vendor is blocked" is a field error even though only the
      server could know it.</li>
      <li><strong>Is it a condition of the document or the system?</strong>
      Then it is a strip: it explains why an action is unavailable, and the
      user's next step is elsewhere (a different period, a different
      approver, waiting).</li>
      <li><strong>Say what is still possible.</strong> A strip that only says
      "closed" invites a support call; one that says "you can still save,
      just not post" does not.</li>
      <li><strong>One strip, not a feed.</strong> If two document-level
      conditions hold, the one that blocks the user's likely next action goes
      first — and if you find yourself needing scroll, paging or filters in
      here, the screen is doing too much. That is the signal to split the
      screen, not to build a message centre.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Deliberately not a message centre.</strong>
    A collected inbox of everything the server wanted to say is what a screen
    grows when errors sit far from where the user is looking. Field-first
    keeps them where the eyes already are; a document strip is the narrow
    exception for messages that have no field to sit on. The framework ships
    no message-manager component, and that is a boundary rather than a gap.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>States</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">What ships</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>No errors</td><td>the summary is absent from the DOM, not hidden — an empty alert region confuses assistive tech</td></tr>
          <tr><td>One error</td><td>still use the summary; the consistent path beats a special case</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Many errors</td><td>list all of them, each linking to its field; do not truncate</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Server-only error</td><td>e.g. "vendor is blocked" — the summary is the ONLY place it can appear</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Fixed as you go</td><td>remove entries as fields become valid, but keep the summary until resubmit &mdash; the count is the user&rsquo;s progress bar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cross-field error</td><td>attach it to the field the user should change, not to a phantom "form" field</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Components used</h2>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><span class="bo-badge">complexity 2 of 4</span></p>
    <ul class="bo-cluster docs-list-bare">
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/alerts'}>Alerts</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/components/form'}>Forms</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/concepts/js-behaviors'}>initValidationSummary()</a></li>
      <li><a class="bo-badge bo-badge--type" href={base + '/patterns/detail-form'}>Detail form</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ["/components/alerts", "Alerts & toasts"],
    ["/components/form", "Forms"],
    ["/concepts/js-behaviors", "JS behaviors"],
    ["/patterns/login", "Login pattern"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
